---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Learning Rust while the agents write it — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="I picked Rust knowing only the basics, and I still write very little of it by hand. Here's the loop I run around the agents instead, and the four ways it goes wrong."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-02">July 2, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Learning Rust while the agents write it</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          I picked Rust knowing only the fundamentals. Here's the loop I run around the agents instead, and the four ways it goes wrong.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 8 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          My actual job is the loop around the agents. I draft the plan, have the coding agent improve it, let a different coding agents build it, a different agent prove it works by reviewing it, running it and parsing the logs.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <p>
        If you're working this way too, or thinking about it, here's what I'd
        want you to know before you start.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Write the plan, including the proof.</strong> If I can't say
          what would show the change worked, the agent will happily pick its
          own definition of done, and it will be generous with itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Be the witness.</strong> "Should now be faster" is not a
          result. A number I watched get produced is. This one sentence has
          saved me more time than anything else on the list.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Review with a reader that has no history.</strong> Whatever
          wrote the code is the worst judge of it, and also the most agreeable.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One change at a time.</strong> It costs almost nothing, and
          it preserves the only clue worth having when something breaks, which
          is knowing which change did it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Learn the language anyway.</strong> Not so I can type faster.
          So I can tell good from merely plausible.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>I took on two problems at once</h2>
      <p>
        A few days ago I wrote about
        <a href="/blogs/why-rust.html">choosing Rust for the whole app</a>.
        What I skipped in that post is how much Rust I actually knew when I
        chose it, which was the basics. I'd shipped Rust before, so I wasn't
        starting from zero. But the basics are a long way from a real-time
        image pipeline that has to keep up with a slider, and I knew that gap
        was going to hurt.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second problem is the goal itself. I don't want a photo editor that
        merely works. I want one where you move a slider and the image has
        already changed, on a folder with thousands of RAW files in it. That's
        a performance target, and performance is the one thing you can't bolt
        on at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        Taking on both at once is normally how a side project quietly dies.
        What makes it survivable is that I'm not the one typing.
      </p>

      <h2>Where my time actually goes</h2>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 230" role="img" aria-label="Stacked bars for five stages of the loop. Planning is almost entirely my own work. Building and releasing are almost entirely the agents. Testing and review sit in between, with the agents doing much of the work and me checking it.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHO DOES THE WORK, BY STAGE</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="44">Plan</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="30" width="470" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="566" y="30" width="42" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="104" y="44">me, and I don't hand this one over</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="80">Build</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="66" width="40" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="136" y="66" width="472" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="144" y="80">the agents write essentially all of it</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="116">Test</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="102" width="240" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="336" y="102" width="272" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="104" y="116">me, running the real app</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="152">Review</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="138" width="180" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="276" y="138" width="332" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="104" y="152">me, reading the diff</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="188">Release</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="96" y="174" width="24" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="174" width="488" height="20" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="128" y="188">scripted, and safe to run twice</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="96" y1="204" x2="608" y2="204" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="96" y="222">BLUE IS MY OWN HANDS ON THE KEYBOARD</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The agents do nearly all of the typing. The first stage is the one
          I keep for myself, and it's the one that decides everything after
          it.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Planning is the part I won't hand over</h2>
      <p>
        Every change starts as a short written plan, and I write it. Not a
        document, not a spec with headings. Usually a paragraph and a list. It
        says what the change is, which parts of the app it touches, what's
        deliberately out of scope, and how I'll know it worked.
      </p>
      <p>
        That last item does most of the work. If I can't say what would prove
        the change is done, I don't have a plan, I have a wish. "Make
        thumbnails faster" is a wish. "A folder of 3,000 photos fills the first
        two screens without the scroll stuttering, and memory doesn't climb
        while I scroll" is a plan.
      </p>
      <p>
        Planning first is also the cheapest possible place to be wrong. An
        agent will confidently build something completely the wrong shape.
        Catching that in a paragraph costs me a minute. Catching it in a branch
        costs an afternoon, and honestly I probably won't throw the branch
        away, because by then it compiles and I'm attached to it.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        The agent's wrong assumption is free while it's still a sentence. Once
        it's code that compiles, I start defending it.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        This is the same discipline as the seven-item list in
        <a href="/blogs/why-i-started-lightphotos.html">the first post</a>. It
        turns out writing the list up front matters more, not less, when
        something can implement the whole list in twenty minutes.
      </p>

      <h2>Building one thing at a time</h2>
      <p>
        The rule I keep coming back to is that a change has to end somewhere I
        can stand. One task, then build, then run the app, then the next task.
        Not five tasks and a hopeful build at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        This isn't about tidiness. When two changes land together and the app
        misbehaves, I've lost the one piece of information I care about, which
        is which change did it. Agents are very good at producing a lot of
        plausible code quickly, which makes them very good at destroying that
        information if I let them.
      </p>

      <h2>The agent can't be the only witness</h2>
      <p>
        Agents are excellent at announcing success. "The thumbnail loading
        should now be much faster." Should. That one word has cost me more time
        than any bug in this codebase.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the pixel maths has real tests, because it's the easy case. Exposure,
        the tone curve, white balance and the rest are pure functions, and a
        pure function is something you can pin down with numbers that don't
        move.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything else I check by running the app myself, and this is where
        I'm weakest right now. Scrolling, decoding, panning, memory. Clicking
        around a window tells me whether something is broken. It doesn't
        reliably tell me whether a change made things faster or slower, because
        I never click the same way twice.
      </p>
      <p>
        I don't have a good answer for that yet. It's the next thing I want to
        fix, and I expect it'll be its own post. In the meantime my working
        rule is that it works when I ran it, not when the agent says so.
      </p>

      <h2>A reader that didn't write the code</h2>
      <p>
        When a change is done, a fresh agent reviews it. One with no memory of
        the conversation that produced it, told to go and find what's wrong
        with the diff.
      </p>
      <p>
        That framing matters more than I expected. The agent that just wrote
        four hundred lines is the worst possible judge of them, and it'll agree
        with you enthusiastically whichever way you lean.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 200" role="img" aria-label="Two reviewers compared. The agent that wrote the code agrees with you warmly and cannot judge its own four hundred lines. A fresh agent with no memory keeps finding the same five things: a clone to dodge the borrow checker, an allocation in a per-pixel loop, an unwrap on user input, a lock held during slow work, and an unasked-for refactor.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHO SHOULD REVIEW THE DIFF</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="300" height="168" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="46">The agent that wrote it</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="16" y="58" width="268" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="150" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Agrees with you, warmly</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="112">It just wrote four hundred lines.</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="132">It is the worst possible judge</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="152">of whether they are any good.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="320" y="24" width="320" height="168" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="336" y="46">A fresh agent, no memory</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="62" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="70">Clone to dodge the borrow checker</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="84" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="92">Allocation in a per-pixel loop</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="106" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="114">An unwrap on user input</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="128" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="136">A lock held during slow work</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="336" y="150" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="352" y="158">An unasked-for refactor</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The findings on the right are boringly consistent, and almost all of
          them are about waste rather than correctness.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Those five come up again and again. Cloning is the easiest way to make
        Rust stop complaining, and on a 60 MB image buffer in a per-frame
        function that's a disaster that compiles cleanly. Allocating inside a
        per-pixel loop looks perfectly reasonable right up until it runs twenty
        million times. A lock held during slow work is what turns a background
        thread into a stalled interface.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I read the diff myself. Not to catch what the reviewer missed,
        because mostly I can't. I do it because this turned out to be how I
        learned Rust.
      </p>

      <h2>Making releases boring on purpose</h2>
      <p>
        A release is a version bump, a build for each target, and the site
        updated with the new build. It's scripted, and it's safe to run twice.
        That second property is the one I keep deliberately. A release I have
        to concentrate on is a release I'll put off, and then I'm sitting on a
        month of unshipped work.
      </p>

      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Stage</th>
              <th scope="col">What I hand over</th>
              <th scope="col">Done when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Plan</th>
              <td>A paragraph and a list</td>
              <td>I can state what would prove it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Build</th>
              <td>One task, with its scope</td>
              <td>It compiles and the app still runs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Test</th>
              <td>The proof from the plan</td>
              <td>I saw it, on a real folder</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Review</th>
              <td>The diff, to an agent that didn't write it</td>
              <td>Findings are fixed or consciously declined</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Release</th>
              <td>A version number</td>
              <td>The build on the site is the build I tested</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Four ways this goes wrong</h2>
      <p>
        I don't want to make this sound smoother than it is. Here's where it
        falls over.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Whack-a-mole.</strong> A symptom gets fixed, another appears,
        and the agent cheerfully fixes that one too. Four rounds in, the code
        is a pile of guards and nobody has said out loud what's actually wrong.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 212" role="img" aria-label="Symptom chasing runs symptom, guard, symptom, guard, symptom, guard, and four rounds in nobody has said what is actually wrong. What works instead is to reproduce it, make the agent name the cause, then apply one fix.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WHEN AN AGENT FIXES SYMPTOMS</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="640" height="92" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="44">Symptom chasing</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="16" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="62" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Symptom</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="116" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="162" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Guard</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="216" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="262" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Symptom</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="316" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="362" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Guard</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="416" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="462" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Symptom</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="516" y="56" width="92" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="562" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Guard</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="16" y="104">Four rounds in, nobody has said what is actually wrong.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="128" width="640" height="76" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="148">What works instead</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="160" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="180" text-anchor="middle">Reproduce it</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="175" x2="214" y2="175"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 170 L224 175 L214 180 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="160" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="180" text-anchor="middle">Name the cause</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="175" x2="422" y2="175"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 170 L432 175 L422 180 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="432" y="160" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="180" text-anchor="middle">One fix</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The only thing that works is to stop, reproduce the problem, and make
          the agent state the cause before it's allowed to touch a line.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <strong>Confident numbers that were never measured.</strong> I've been
        told a change made something "roughly three times faster" by an agent
        that ran nothing at all. Any number I didn't watch get produced is a
        guess wearing a number's clothes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Code that is correct and wasteful.</strong> Rust's compiler is
        a strict reviewer, and it made me braver than I should have been. It
        checks that you aren't doing anything unsafe. It has no opinion
        whatsoever about whether you just copied fifty megabytes for no reason.
        For an app whose whole premise is speed, that's a gap I have to cover
        myself, and it's why performance is a gate in the loop rather than
        something I hope for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Scope drift.</strong> Left unattended, a change grows. Naming
        what's out of scope in the plan handles most of it, and reading the
        diff handles the rest.
      </p>

      <h2>So how did I actually learn Rust?</h2>
      <p>
        Not from a book, and not by writing an app from scratch. Four things
        did it, roughly in order of how much they taught me.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Reading diffs with one question.</strong> Why is this line
          here? Asked a few hundred times, across real code doing something I
          care about.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Asking for justifications.</strong> Why a clone here, why a
          reference there, why <code>Arc</code> and not a plain value. Those
          answers are where ownership stopped being a rule I'd read about and
          became something I could predict.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Borrow checker errors.</strong> A tutor with no patience and
          no small talk, which turns out to be a surprisingly effective
          combination.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Writing some of it by hand anyway.</strong> A function per
          session, no agent. Slow, and the only way I found out what I actually
          understood versus what I only recognised.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A few weeks in, I can read all of the Rust in LightPhotos and write
        most of it. Reading is the half that matters for this loop.
      </p>
      <p>
        An agent can always produce more code than I can write. What it can't
        give me is the judgment to tell whether the code it produced is any
        good, and that judgment is the only thing standing between "it works"
        and the app I'm actually trying to build.
      </p>


